					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/presque" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3626022); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3626022&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606984926" alt="Presque" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3626022)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/presque" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3626022); return false;" >Presque</a></span> <span class="odate time_1527151073 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 May 2018 08:37</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3811058">
									

<p>Disclaimer: I come back to this article every few days so it and the followup don't expire in my mental checklist.</p>
<p>The irony is that this is an example of what any normal person could do with a package sent to the wrong place- not much beyond sending it back. Social expectations would usually demand that you don't try to find its &quot;story&quot; from the return address, assuming there is one.</p>
<p>And you're only able to just assume the mundane, if you even think about it at all.</p>
<p>Basically the same thing for anything second-hand, or abandoned on the side of the road or decaying in a junkyard.</p>
<p>But there is also another possibility: maybe they figured or found out something, and this article isn't telling the whole truth?</p>
<p>Maybe it's intended to manipulate the subject or subjects, maybe the researchers perspective&#8230;maybe the reader. Or maybe all three.</p>
<p>Wouldn't it be more convenient for some &quot;randomly&quot; placed silverware to be stolen than your keys? What would a &quot;hallucination&quot; want more: a fork, or your attention?</p>
<p>While it is more satisfying to have an answer, we don't always have the entitlement to get that. At least, not immediately.</p>
<p>One definite thing: compartmentalization seems to be almost as popular as it is effective.</p>
<p>Another definite thing: there are many sides to this and, depending on personal definitions and interests, probably just as many stories (anecdotes?) that may or may not be relevant.</p>
<p>Godsleap, or Godsleep?</p>

							</div>

								
